From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] explorer: The Task View button is painted where it moves (6529)

When a notification-area icon goes away, the Task View button moves with
the desktop pager. Only the pager and the search button were invalidated
after the taskbar laid them out, so the button repainted just the strip
it newly covered. On qa4 it showed a cut-off fragment until it was
hovered. The button is now invalidated after it is placed, as the pager
is, and so is the Start button, whose width follows the look.
---
diff --git a/programs/explorer/systray.c b/programs/explorer/systray.c
index 37c6f77..dd67f3f 100644
--- a/programs/explorer/systray.c
+++ b/programs/explorer/systray.c
@@ -1497,6 +1497,7 @@ static void sync_taskbar_buttons(void)
     if (start)
     {
         place_button( start->button, pos, sg_start_len() );
+        InvalidateRect( start->button, NULL, TRUE );   /* (wine-sg 6529: its width follows the look) */
         pos += sg_start_len();
     }
     if (search_button)
@@ -1511,7 +1512,15 @@ static void sync_taskbar_buttons(void)
     }
     if (taskview_button)
     {
-        if (sg_bar.taskview) place_button( taskview_button, limit, SG_TASKVIEW_WIDTH );
+        if (sg_bar.taskview)
+        {
+            /* Stained Glass OS (wine-sg 6529): it moves with the notification area (a tray icon
+             * that comes or goes): painted again where it now is, as the pager is */
+            place_button( taskview_button, limit, SG_TASKVIEW_WIDTH );
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_TASKVIEW_NO_REPAINT
+            InvalidateRect( taskview_button, NULL, TRUE );
+#endif
+        }
         else hide_button( taskview_button );
     }
     if (pager_window && sg_bar.pager)
